<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
	xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
	<modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

	<parent>
		<groupId>dev.dimster</groupId>
		<artifactId>dimster</artifactId>
		<version>0.5.0</version>
		<relativePath>..</relativePath>
	</parent>

	<artifactId>dimster-spi</artifactId>
	<name>Dimster SPI</name>
	<packaging>jar</packaging>

	<!--
	Service Provider Interface for dimster's Kafka client layer.

	Why this module exists:
	The Kafka client API surface differs between releases - share-group APIs
	(KafkaShareConsumer, AcknowledgeType, ListShareGroupOffsetsSpec, GroupType.SHARE,
	alterShareGroupOffsets, ...) only exist in 4.2+, and even between point releases
	such as 4.2.0 vs 4.2.1, signatures drift. To compile and benchmark against multiple
	Kafka versions from the same dimster-core source tree, we extract the narrow
	version-touching surface into this SPI. dimster-core depends only on this module;
	per-version impl modules (dimster-kafka-3.7, dimster-kafka-4.2.0, ...) provide
	concrete implementations that delegate to their respective kafka-clients version.
	The right impl is bound at startup via java.util.ServiceLoader.

	Why kafka-clients is a compile-scope dep pinned to the OLDEST supported
	version (3.7.x):
	The SPI deliberately reuses Kafka's stable value types (TopicPartition,
	OffsetAndMetadata, Metric, MetricName, ProducerRecord, RecordMetadata, NewTopic,
	Callback) instead of inventing parallel mirror types. These have been API-stable
	across many Kafka releases. Pinning the SPI's own dep to 3.7.x is what
	guarantees we never accidentally reference a type that doesn't exist in the
	oldest version we support - the dimster-spi module would refuse to compile if
	someone added a 4.2-only type to an interface signature.

	The compile scope means kafka-clients 3.7.x transitively flows to dimster-core
	(which has no direct kafka-clients dep of its own), giving its wrapper code a
	stable subset of types to compile against. At runtime in a per-flavor package
	(e.g. package-4.2.0), the per-version impl module declares its own
	kafka-clients at compile scope at depth 2 (e.g. 4.2.0); the spi-transitive
	3.7.x is at depth 3. Maven's nearest-wins resolution picks the depth-2 version,
	so the runtime classpath gets exactly one kafka-clients (the per-flavor one)
	while compile-time only sees the 3.7.x stable subset.
	-->

	<properties>
		<!-- Oldest Kafka client version this SPI supports. Anything in the SPI must
		     compile against this version; if a 4.2+ type slips into a signature, the
		     module fails to build, which is the early-warning we want. -->
		<kafka.spi.baseline.version>3.7.2</kafka.spi.baseline.version>
	</properties>

	<dependencies>
		<dependency>
			<groupId>org.apache.kafka</groupId>
			<artifactId>kafka-clients</artifactId>
			<version>${kafka.spi.baseline.version}</version>
		</dependency>
	</dependencies>

</project>
